evidence:
crates/fleet-app/src/bridge/runtime.rs:96-108, inside the only `select!` arm that reads control commands:
```rust
tokio::select! {
    command = commands.recv() => match command {
        Ok(Command::Request { body, reply }) => {
            if opening.is_some() {
                queue_while_opening(&mut waiting, body, reply, resync_pending);
            } else if requests.send(requests::Request::Command {
                client: link.as_ref().map(|link| link.client.clone()), body, reply,
            }).await.is_err() {          // <- parks the whole select, Shutdown included
                return;
            }
        }
```
`requests` is `async_channel::bounded(COMMAND_CAPACITY)` (runtime.rs:77, `COMMAND_CAPACITY = 512`, bridge.rs:53). Its consumer parks on the next hop for reply-less work — crates/fleet-app/src/bridge/requests.rs:22 and :36:
```rust
let (mutations, mutation_rx) = async_channel::bounded(COMMAND_CAPACITY);
…
None => { if mutations.send(Mutation { client, body }).await.is_err() { … } }
```
and the mutation worker is strictly serial with no bound of its own — requests.rs:50-59:
```rust
while let Ok(Mutation { client, body }) = mutations.recv().await {
    if let Some(client) = client {
        if let Err(error) = client.request(*body).await { … }
```
`client.request` costs up to `REQUEST_TIMEOUT = 10 s` per body (crates/fleet-client/src/connection.rs:35, :590-609). Note the loop's own `select!` carries no `biased;`, so even when it is runnable `Shutdown` only wins a coin flip against the ready health ticker. Contrast the shed-load policy the same file already implements for its other full-queue case, `queue_while_opening` (runtime.rs:220-228) and `Bridge::send` (bridge.rs:368-377), which set `resync_pending` and report a visible mutation failure rather than blocking.

